    dict(spec="0022", round=6, kind="external", date="2026-08-17", verdict="RETURN FOR AMENDMENT (2 blocking on this spec + 2 package/process; 0023 semantically clear but deferred by its incomplete ledger and the atomic dependency; 0004 not reopened). R6-4 — THE OFFLINE LAUNCHER CERTIFIED AN UNQUALIFIED RUNTIME. It invented its own rule (SQLite >= 3.35) while the repository already implements the real predicate, runtime_supported(), derived from recorded runtimes and reproduced constructor manifestations (0007). Measured: the launcher selected SQLite 3.53.1, called it qualified, and the suite produced 660 FAILED / 951 passed / 31 errors while runtime_supported() returned False for that runtime. It asks the repository now and REFUSES with exit 2, proven by forcing the predicate False on a scratch copy. R6-1 — _rollback_or_poison caught Exception while the operation caught BaseException, so a KeyboardInterrupt during ROLLBACK escaped as itself: caller told nothing, connection open and in_transaction, uncommitted row surviving. The narrower boundary was a hole in the wider one's guarantee. Aligned, with the reviewer's injection as a regression. R6-2 — §4e-i's block is GENERATED byte-for-byte from the executable and the page still carried round 5's 'verbatim is withdrawn, the executable differs materially'. Three carriers, two answers; the withdrawal is deleted. R6-3 — the closure ledger was a HAND-MAINTAINED SECOND LIST, the very thing render_closure was introduced to eliminate, and it was 12/17 and 3/9 complete while --check stayed green because it compared the block to that same incomplete list. Finding ids are now EXTRACTED from reviews.py's verdict text and every one must have a row; 26 findings, all with runnable evidence, and 0023 F1's command no longer prints the unmodified consumers (it demonstrated the defect, not its closure)", raised=['R6-1', 'R6-2', 'R6-3', 'R6-4'], findings=4),
