    dict(spec="0022", round=5, kind="external", date="2026-08-17", verdict="RETURN FOR AMENDMENT (2 blocking on this spec + 2 package/process; 0023 semantically clear, deferred only by the mutual requires; 0004 not reopened). R5-1 — the failure outcomes were NOT TOTAL: a failing ROLLBACK was suppressed and the ORIGINAL error re-raised, so the caller held a live transaction and an uncommitted row it had been told was undone; and EVERY sqlite3.IntegrityError became OrdinalCollision, so a trigger on the records table reported a serialisation failure that had not happened. Three distinct outcomes now, with the ordinal matched on its own constraint columns, and an unknown-state rollback CLOSES the connection. R5-2 — two claimed regressions did not exercise their branches. Instrumented, the BUSY check made ONE BEGIN IMMEDIATE and caught ZERO OperationalErrors: busy_timeout=5000 meant SQLite waited internally and the assertion ('it waited >0.1s') was satisfied by the C library, not the Python loop it was named for — A TIMING ASSERTION CANNOT TELL YOU WHO WAITED. The collision check raised OrdinalCollision by hand and never passed one through the operation. Rewritten with busy_timeout=0 and a counter on the loop's own attempts, plus a bounded-deadline check. **AND THE REWRITE ESTABLISHED SOMETHING THE SPEC DID NOT EXPECT: a stale ordinal CANNOT be injected inside the operation.** While it holds the write lock nothing else can COMMIT, so OrdinalCollision is UNREACHABLE from inside the construction — it is a backstop for callers who allocate outside the transaction. Proven as its own check; the classifier is covered directly on real errors rather than through a branch the design makes dead. 'Quoted verbatim' is WITHDRAWN — the spec block and the executable differ by test hooks, helper names and return shape, and claiming byte-identity is the same defect one level up. R5-3 — the generated closure was one row per ROUND with a 300-char verdict; PROCESS §4a requires one row per FINDING with openable evidence, and a SECOND hand-written table still sat at the top of 0022, stale before external rounds 3-4, unguarded by --check. Findings are now structural (specs/closure_findings.py, 15 rows, each with a runnable command) and the hand-written table is deleted. R5-4 — reconcile() matched pytest's EMITTED reason against SOURCE-SITE tokens, so the euid skip read as unlisted on a ROOT host and only there. EMITTED vocabulary separated, with a root-host regression that would have failed on the reviewer's host and passed on ours", raised=['R5-1', 'R5-2', 'R5-3', 'R5-4'], findings=4),
