    dict(spec="0022", round=4, kind="external", date="2026-08-17", verdict="RETURN FOR AMENDMENT (1 blocking on this spec + 2 package/process; 0004 not reopened). R4-1 — `revocation_operation` was NEITHER ATOMIC NOR ACTUALLY SHARED. The reviewer invoked the submitted function directly and got `applied effects: []`: it appended the row and never applied the effects, so R19's 'the row and the effects land together' was true of the row alone. It also DISCARDED `reason` and `at` (the append hard-coded both, so the audit trail recorded defaults instead of the operator's words), declared `plan=None` while the spec called `plan(standing)` unconditionally, and the BUSY regression exercised a SEPARATE helper rather than the function under test. **The harness scored 7/7 on an operation that applied nothing, because every check asked about ordinals and rows and none asked whether the work happened.** Fixed: the operation applies every effect inside the same transaction, stores the operator's metadata, requires `plan`, and rolls the ROW back with the effects on any fault; six new checks cover effect landing, mid-effect rollback, absent-record rollback, metadata, BUSY through the function, and collision non-retry — and reverting to the old behaviour FAILS two of them. R4-3 — the closure ledgers had drifted for a THIRD round; they are now GENERATED from reviews.py by specs/render_closure.py with a --check gate, and the generator distinguishes SENT dispatch rows from returned verdicts so a package dispatch is never counted as a round. R4-4 — skip_inventory.render()'s category list was HARD-CODED and omitted `future-obligation`, so the four entries added at round 3 never reached the rendered block; the block still recited a 14-skip decomposition beside a measured line of 6 and cited a removed pydantic regression; and no shipped test exercised reconcile(). Fixed: the renderer RAISES on any category it would drop, the decomposition is COMPUTED from the sealed -rs output, and three tests now ship (unlisted skip, truncated report, dropped category)", raised=['R4-1', 'R4-3', 'R4-4'], findings=3),
